      'Emmet Fox was an Irish-born engineer who became one of the most widely heard preachers in America. Through the 1930s he filled the largest halls in New York — Carnegie Hall among them — with midweek services that drew thousands during the Depression. His writing keeps a certain engineering plainness: define the problem, give the procedure, explain what will go wrong if the procedure is not followed.',
