    note: 'Quimby published nothing in his lifetime. What survives is a body of unpublished papers, edited and released decades after his death, whose arrangement and wording have been disputed — particularly in the long controversy over his influence on Mary Baker Eddy. His nineteenth-century healing accounts are anecdotal and were recorded without any medical documentation; they belong to the history of ideas, not to clinical evidence.',
    overview: [
      'Phineas Parkhurst Quimby was a clockmaker in Belfast, Maine, who became interested in mesmerism in the 1830s, practised it, and then abandoned it — concluding that whatever helped his patients had nothing to do with magnetic fluid and everything to do with what they believed. For the last fifteen years of his life he saw patients in Portland, working by conversation, and wrote continuously without publishing a word.',
      'His question was why people got better. The answer he settled on was that a great deal of suffering is organised around an explanation the patient has accepted — often supplied by a doctor or a preacher — and that changing the explanation changes what the person experiences. He called the wrong explanation "error" and the correct one "the Truth" or "Wisdom," and he understood his work as removing the first rather than administering anything.',
      'Everything downstream in this section of the library begins here: the mind-cure movement, the New Thought denominations, the language of belief and healing. Quimby himself is more modest than his successors. He is describing what he observed in a consulting room in the 1860s, before germ theory was established, without a laboratory, and his surviving papers are exploratory rather than doctrinal.',
    ],
