    note: 'The Secret presents the law of attraction as a universal law comparable to gravity and invokes quantum physics in support. That framing is not accepted by physics and has been widely criticised, including for passages implying that misfortune is attracted by those who suffer it. The gratitude and visualisation practices are benign in themselves; the causal claims made for them are not established.',
    overview: [
      'Rhonda Byrne is an Australian television producer who, after a difficult period in her own life, encountered Wallace Wattles\' 1910 book The Science of Getting Rich and set out to gather the teachers who shared its outlook into a film. The Secret appeared in 2006 as a documentary and then a book, and became one of the best-selling self-help properties of the century, translated into dozens of languages.',
      'Her contribution is not doctrinal — almost every idea in The Secret can be found a century earlier in Wattles, Haanel, Collier or Troward. What she supplied was packaging and reach: a simple three-step formula, a confident tone, and a production that put the material in front of an audience that had never heard of New Thought. For a great many contemporary readers, this book is where the tradition begins.',
      'It is also the most criticised item in this library, and fairly so in places. The film\'s presentation of attraction as a physical law, its appeals to quantum physics, and passages implying that catastrophe befalls those on the wrong frequency drew sustained objection from scientists, clinicians and religious writers alike. Her later books moved towards gratitude and love as the central practice, which is gentler ground and, in practice, where most readers find the value.',
    ],
    context: {
      period: 'The film and book appeared in 2006; the follow-up books through the 2010s.',
      tradition: 'Law of attraction — a popular restatement of early twentieth-century New Thought for a mass contemporary audience.',
      influences: 'Wallace Wattles directly and by her own account; Charles Haanel, Robert Collier, Genevieve Behrend and the classic prosperity literature; and the contemporary teachers who appear in the film.',
      influenced: 'The entire modern manifestation industry — vision boards, scripting, "high vibration" language — descends more from The Secret than from any single earlier source.',
      imageVsRecord: 'The book is often taken as the origin of these ideas. It is a repackaging of a century-old literature, and the older sources are frequently more careful about action and effort than the film is.',
    },
