      'Jiddu Krishnamurti was discovered as a boy in India by leaders of the Theosophical Society, raised and educated to be the vehicle of a coming world teacher, and placed at the head of an international organisation built for that purpose. In 1929, at thirty-four, he dissolved it in front of its members, returned its properties and funds, and refused the role — saying that truth is a pathless land which cannot be approached by any organisation, creed or teacher.',
      'He spent the next fifty-seven years talking, in Europe, India and America, about a single subject: the possibility of a mind free from its own conditioning. Not free through a technique — he regarded all techniques as further conditioning — but through a quality of direct, immediate observation of how fear, desire, comparison and thought actually operate in oneself, watched as they happen.',
      'This makes him the most awkward figure in any library of teachers, including this one. He was consistently hostile to the entire apparatus of spiritual authority, including his own, and refused to give methods, practices or reassurance. What he offers instead is a relentless quality of attention to one\'s own mind — and a body of talks that resist being turned into a system, largely because he spent his life ensuring they would.',
