      title: 'Applied to anxiety about an uncertain future',
      scenario: 'Someone with an unstable freelance income spends most of their mental life trying to secure the next twelve months — modelling scenarios, rehearsing failure, seeking a certainty the work does not offer. Watts\' argument is not that this is irrational but that it is impossible: the security being sought does not exist for anyone, employed or not, and the search itself is what is producing most of the suffering. The practical move is not to stop planning — he was not against sensible arrangements — but to notice the difference between the twenty minutes of planning that helps and the six hours of grasping that follow. What he offers in place of the six hours is not reassurance but a change of premise: a life is not a problem awaiting solution, and insecurity is the condition of being alive rather than evidence that something has gone wrong.',
