      'His target is a Christendom in which everyone is nominally Christian and almost nobody has had to choose anything. Against this he sets the single individual: the person who cannot hide in the crowd, the age, or the system, and who must decide how to exist. Truth, for him, is not only a matter of getting propositions right but of the relation in which one holds them — a truth one admires without living is, in his terms, not yet possessed.',
      'This makes him the sharpest available critic of comfortable religion and of comfortable spirituality alike. He is also the founding voice of existential thought, and his analyses of anxiety and despair anticipate a great deal of later psychology: anxiety, he argued, is the dizziness of freedom — not a malfunction but the felt sensation of having possibilities.',
