      'Earl Nightingale served in the US Navy and survived the attack on Pearl Harbor, worked as a radio announcer, and in 1956 recorded a short talk for his insurance sales team that he called The Strangest Secret. It sold over a million copies on vinyl, became the first spoken-word recording to earn a gold record, and effectively created the audio self-development industry. He went on to co-found Nightingale-Conant, whose cassette programmes shaped the field for decades.',
      'The talk\'s premise is a single sentence: we become what we think about. He arrived at it from the classical success literature — Hill above all — and from a habit of reading widely in a period when he was, by his own account, going nowhere and did not know why some people succeeded and others did not.',
