      'His teaching is presence joined to love. Where much non-dual instruction can be dry, his is devotional: the point of stepping out of the thinking mind is not detachment but the capacity to meet another person with an open heart. His formulation of the practice — I am loving awareness — is deliberately simple and repeatable, and it holds the two halves of his path together.',
      'The last quarter of his life gave the teaching its authority. A severe stroke in 1997 left him with aphasia and in a wheelchair, and he spent the following two decades teaching from that condition, calling it fierce grace. His late work on ageing, illness and dying is his most substantial, and it is where the warmth stops being charming and becomes hard-won.',
