      scenario: 'Someone is sitting with a parent in their final months and finds themselves managing — arranging care, researching options, keeping busy so as not to feel it. Ram Dass\'s late work speaks directly to this. His counsel would be to keep doing the practical things and to stop using them as a shield: to come into the room as loving awareness rather than as the coordinator, to be present with the person rather than with the illness, and to allow that this is unbearable without needing to make it something else. He also insisted on the helper\'s side of it — that exhaustion and resentment are ordinary, that pretending otherwise is a burden, and that being with someone honestly is worth more than being with them serenely.',
