      'Thich Nhat Hanh was a Vietnamese Zen monk who ordained at sixteen and spent the war years organising relief work, rebuilding bombed villages and training young people in what he called engaged Buddhism — practice that does not withdraw from the world\'s suffering. He was exiled in 1966 after travelling to call for peace, and did not return to Vietnam for nearly four decades. Martin Luther King Jr nominated him for the Nobel Peace Prize in 1967.',
