      scenario: 'Someone is carrying anger towards a family member whose behaviour will not alter. Suppressing it has produced tension; expressing it has produced arguments. Thich Nhat Hanh offers a third path that he practised on far worse material. Sit with the anger and breathe with it, acknowledging it kindly as one would a distressed child — not analysing it, not justifying it, not sending it away. Then look at the conditions that produced the other person: their own history, their own fear, the causes they did not choose. He would not ask for immediate forgiveness, and he would not ask for the anger to be denied. He would ask for it to be tended until it can be looked at, and for whatever action follows to come from that state rather than from the heat.',
