      'This biography is inseparable from the teaching. A man who had been owned taught that freedom is not a matter of circumstance: what can be taken from you was never yours, and what is genuinely yours — your judgements, your intentions, your responses — cannot be seized by anyone. It is a doctrine with unusual authority behind it.',
      'His method is severe and practical. He is not interested in metaphysics for its own sake, he is impatient with students who admire philosophy without practising it, and his standard question is what you actually did when something went wrong. The Enchiridion, a short handbook of his key points, is among the most concentrated practical documents in Western thought.',
