      'His most useful ideas are two. First, that we suffer far more in imagination than in reality — that most of what torments us is anticipated rather than present. Second, that time is the only genuinely non-renewable possession, and that people who would guard a coin will give away an afternoon to anyone who asks. On the Shortness of Life makes the case with a directness that has not aged in two thousand years.',
