      'Adyashanti — born Steven Gray in California — practised Zen for fourteen years under Arvis Justi, a teacher in the Maezumi lineage, and began teaching at her request in 1996. He uses no Japanese, wears no robes, holds no formal transmission credentials that he emphasises, and describes his teaching as drawing on both Zen and Advaita without belonging to either.',
      'His central concern is unusually specific: not awakening itself but what happens afterwards. He speaks openly about the period following an initial opening — the disorientation, the reassertion of old patterns, the temptation to build a spiritual identity out of the experience — and treats embodiment, the slow working of realisation into an actual personality and life, as the harder and longer part.',
      'The practice he teaches is a subtraction. True meditation, in his phrasing, means giving up control of attention entirely: not concentrating, not observing, not managing the mind, but allowing everything to be exactly as it is and noticing what is already aware. It is the least effortful instruction in this section and, for people habituated to spiritual technique, among the most difficult.',
